m=test.shared.f64boundary;

i=t:std.test;
i=str:std.str;
i=json:std.json;
i=sh:shared.types;

(* Where an f64 stops being itself.

   T-20 in docs/upstream/toke-issues.md records that an f64 RETURNED across a module
   boundary is corrupted: shared.types.f64abs(-50.0) arrives as 1. f64 parameters cross
   intact; only returns are affected. That finding sized LS1.1h at XL across 23
   functions and is the stated reason no figure this project computes across a module
   boundary can be published.

   It has never been asserted. It was measured once, by hand, against a toke that the
   toolchain has since moved past. So the size of LS1.1h, the size of the ~62-site
   json-on-a-row story, and whether AD1.2's recall floor can be stated at all, all rest
   on a number nobody re-checks.

   This file checks it, in four arms, deliberately ordered so that each one isolates a
   single question. It must IMPORT shared.types — re-declaring f64abs here would move
   the call inside this module and measure nothing, which is the failure mode that makes
   67 of this repository's test files unable to fail.

   MEASURED 2026-09-23 against toke c4e4704. The result, and the mechanism:

     A  a module-local f64 round-trips          PASS  — the harness can observe an f64
     B  sh.f64abs(-50.0) == 50.0                FAIL  — T-20 is live on loke boundaries
     C  json.getfloat over a real JSON object   PASS  — stdlib builtins are EXEMPT
     D  the integer-hundredths workaround       PASS  — the fallback crosses exactly

   So T-20 is narrower than the backlog assumed and its mechanism is now known, which
   between them collapse LS1.1h from XL:

   - `json.getfloat` is exonerated. A stdlib builtin is a `tk_*_w` wrapper that really
     does return the f64 bit pattern in an i64 and really is bitcast back, so both halves
     of that ABI agree. The separate ~62-site defect where a $row is read with a json
     accessor is a DIFFERENT bug (LS1.1i) — a row handle is not a JSON object — and it is
     not about f64 corruption at all.
   - Only loke's own cross-module f64 returns are affected, and the corrupted value is
     not a consistent transform: f64abs(-50.0) arrives as the bit pattern 1 (4.94e-324),
     f64abs(50.0) and f64abs(-1.5) both arrive as 0. The upstream doc's "arrives as 1" was
     one observation of reading the wrong register, not a rule.

   The mechanism, for the upstream report. `llvm.c:1639` declares the intent — "Cross-
   module ABI: all non-void returns use i64 (pointers via ptrtoint, bools via zext,
   floats via bitcast)" — and line 1641 passes a literal "i64", discarding the correct
   "double" that line 1629 just computed via tki_type_to_llvm. But the DEFINITION side
   uses resolve_llvm_type (llvm.c:680), which maps f64 to a real `double`. So
   shared.types emits `define ... double @shared_types_f64abs(double)` while every
   importer emits `declare ... i64 @shared_types_f64abs(double)`. Neither side performs
   the bitcast the comment promises. The callee returns in xmm0, the caller reads rax.
   The PARAMETER is `double` on both sides, which is exactly why f64 parameters cross
   intact and only returns are affected.

   f64hundredths below is the workaround and also the proof: it works only because its
   declared return really is i64, so the two halves agree by construction rather than by
   accident.

   ARM B IS A TRIPWIRE, NOT A FAILING TEST. It asserts that the defect is STILL PRESENT,
   so the suite stays green while the bug stands. The day it fails is the day T-20 is
   fixed upstream — and that is the signal to re-measure, delete the scaled-integer
   workarounds, and close LS1.1h. Do not "fix" arm B by relaxing it. *)

f=arma():bool{
  (* Control. Same-module arithmetic, no boundary crossed. If this fails, the other
     three arms say nothing about T-20 and everything about the harness. *)
  let v=0.0-50.0;
  let abs=mut.v; if(v<0.0){abs=0.0-v}el{};
  <t.assert(str.eq(str.fromfloat(abs);"50.000000") || str.eq(str.fromfloat(abs);"50.0") || str.eq(str.fromfloat(abs);"50");
    "A: a module-local f64 survives (control — if this fails, ignore B/C/D)")
};

f=armb():bool{
  (* The tripwire. shared.types is a different module, so this return crosses a loke
     module boundary and is corrupted. Asserting the DEFECT, not the behaviour we want:
     a green run here means T-20 still stands. See the header before changing this. *)
  let crossed=sh.f64abs(0.0-50.0);
  <t.assert((crossed as i64)!=50;
    "B TRIPWIRE: an f64 returned across a loke module boundary is STILL corrupted (T-20). If this FAILS, T-20 is fixed — re-measure and close LS1.1h")
};

f=armc():bool{
  (* The stdlib question, isolated. json.dec gives a genuine JSON object, so this tests
     a builtin f64 return and NOT the separate defect where a $row is read with a json
     accessor. Builtins cross the C ABI rather than toke's module lowering, so there is
     real reason they may be exempt. *)
  let obj=json.dec("{\"v\":1.5}");
  let v=json.getfloat(obj;"v");
  <t.assert(str.eq(sh.f64fixed(v;1);"1.5");
    "C: f64 returned from a STDLIB builtin is intact (json.getfloat)")
};

f=armd():bool{
  (* The workaround, proven before 62 call sites are committed to it. An integer crosses
     exactly; the caller divides locally. storage/db.tk rowf64hundredths and
     memory/aaak.tk measuresavingsbp are the two functions already migrated this way. *)
  let hundredths=sh.f64hundredths(1.5);
  <t.assert(hundredths==150;
    "D: the integer-hundredths workaround crosses exactly")
};

f=arme():bool{
  (* Arm E, added 2026-09-23. Does an f64 FIELD of a struct returned across a module
     boundary survive, when a bare f64 return does not?

     Asked because core.metrics.collector.summarise returns a $metricssummary with four
     f64 fields and `loke stats` prints all four — so if the answer is no, every figure
     that command displays is wrong, and it would be wrong through a mechanism nobody had
     checked. A struct crosses as a pointer, so the field is loaded from memory rather
     than read out of a return register, and the mechanism that breaks arm B may not
     apply. Measured rather than reasoned about. *)
  let c=sh.makecarrier(12.5);
  <t.assert(str.eq(sh.f64fixed(c.carried;1);"12.5");
    "E: an f64 FIELD of a struct returned across a module boundary is intact")
};

f=armf():bool{
  (* The same struct's integer field, as a control: if E fails and F passes, the struct
     itself crossed fine and only the f64 field is affected. *)
  let c=sh.makecarrier(12.5);
  <t.assert(c.hundredths==1250;
    "F: an i64 field of the same struct is intact (control for E)")
};

f=main():i64{
  let pass=mut.0;
  let fail=mut.0;
  if(arma()){pass=pass+1}el{fail=fail+1};
  if(armb()){pass=pass+1}el{fail=fail+1};
  if(armc()){pass=pass+1}el{fail=fail+1};
  if(armd()){pass=pass+1}el{fail=fail+1};
  if(arme()){pass=pass+1}el{fail=fail+1};
  if(armf()){pass=pass+1}el{fail=fail+1};
  <fail
};
